---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const toc = [
  { label: 'Environment Modules',    id: 'modules' },
  { label: 'Creating an Environment', id: 'create' },
  { label: 'Using It in Jobs',       id: 'activate' },
  { label: 'Reproducibility',        id: 'reproducibility' },
  { label: 'Project Structure',      id: 'structure' },
];
---

<DocsLayout title="Software & Conda" toc={toc}>

  <h1>Software & Conda</h1>

  <p>
    Every project needs its own isolated, reproducible software environment, so that your
    dependencies never interfere with anyone else's, and so that anyone (you six months from now,
    your co-supervisor, a future lab member) can rebuild it from scratch. On polimi-cluster, system
    software is provided through <strong>environment modules</strong>, and Python environments are
    managed with <strong>Conda</strong>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Modules ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="modules">Environment Modules</h2>

  <p>
    Modules make software available to your shell on demand. List what is installed, then load what
    you need:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`module avail                    # list available modules
module load miniconda/25.5.1    # load the cluster's Conda installation
module list                     # show what is currently loaded`}
  />

  <p>
    Loaded modules only apply to the current shell. A Slurm job starts from a fresh environment,
    so <strong>every script must load the modules it needs</strong>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Create ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="create">Creating an Environment</h2>

  <p>
    Installing packages can use considerable CPU, RAM, disk I/O, and network bandwidth, so do not do
    it on the login node. Create Conda environments as Slurm jobs instead. Create
    <code>create-conda-env.sbatch</code>:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="create-conda-env.sbatch"
    code={`#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=create-conda-env
#SBATCH --partition=cpu
#SBATCH --time=02:00:00
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=4
#SBATCH --mem=16G
#SBATCH --output=conda-create-%j.out
#SBATCH --error=conda-create-%j.err

module load miniconda/25.5.1
conda create --yes --name my-project python=3.11`}
  />

  <p>
    Submit it, and check the log once it finishes:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`sbatch create-conda-env.sbatch`}
  />

  <p>
    Use the same pattern to install or update packages. For example, append these lines to the
    script to install your project's dependencies into the new environment:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="create-conda-env.sbatch (continued)"
    code={`conda activate my-project
pip install -r requirements.txt`}
  />

  <!-- ── Activate ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="activate">Using It in Jobs</h2>

  <p>
    Activate the environment in every Slurm script that needs it, before running your code:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="train.sbatch"
    code={`#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH ...                     # resource requests

module load miniconda/25.5.1
conda activate my-project

python train.py`}
  />

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-accent-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/>
      <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/>
    </svg>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      If a job fails with <em>"Run 'conda init' before 'conda activate'"</em>, add
      <code>eval "$(conda shell.bash hook)"</code> right after the <code>module load</code> line.
      Batch scripts are non-interactive shells and do not read the setup that
      <code>conda init</code> writes to <code>~/.bashrc</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Reproducibility ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="reproducibility">Reproducibility</h2>

  <p>
    Keep an environment definition in your project repository, and update it whenever you add or
    upgrade a package:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`conda env export --name my-project > environment.yml`}
  />

  <p>
    Anyone can then rebuild the exact same environment by submitting a job that runs
    <code>conda env create --file environment.yml</code>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Project Structure ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="structure">Project Structure</h2>

  <p>
    Every project should follow a layout like this one. It makes your repository self-contained:
    anyone who clones it can rebuild the environment and run the code without asking you anything.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    filename="my_project/"
    code={`my_project/
├── src/                   # your source code
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── train.py
│   └── evaluate.py
├── configs/               # YAML/JSON configuration files, if any
│   └── default.yaml
├── slurm/                 # your .sbatch scripts
│   ├── create-env.sbatch
│   └── train.sbatch
├── environment.yml        # exported Conda environment
├── .gitignore             # exclude datasets, checkpoints, and *.out / *.err logs
└── README.md              # setup & usage instructions`}
  />

  <p>
    Keep datasets and large checkpoints out of Git: store them in
    <code>~/dataset/private</code> or another directory in your home, as described in
    <a href={`${base}docs/welcome#storage`}>Files &amp; Storage</a>.
  </p>

</DocsLayout>
